  "issue_number": 281,
  "issue_title": "[bug] ensemble.analyze accepts a ragged model pool, so best_single can exceed oracle_any (impossible invariant)",
  "issue_body": "**Describe the bug**\n\n`analysis.ensemble.analyze` (the R3 multi-agent baseline) grades each model over **its own** item count, but the oracle over **all** items:\n\n```python\nfor m, a in answers.items():                    # only the models THIS item names\n    ok = int(score(bench, ..., ref) >= 0.5)\n    per_model_correct.setdefault(m, []).append(ok)   # ragged: lists differ in length\n    ...\noracle.append(1 if any(model_ok) else 0)        # oracle over ALL items\n...\nper_model_acc = {m: sum(v) / len(v) for ...}    # denominator = len(v), NOT the item count n\nbest_single = per_model_acc[best_model]\n```\n\nUnlike every sibling module in this package \u2014 `agreement.grade_items`, `complementarity.solve_matrix_from_matrix`, and `sampling.solve_counts` all **raise `ValueError` on a ragged pool** (agreement's comment: *\"A ragged pool would silently bias every rate below, so it is an error\"*) \u2014 `ensemble.analyze` silently accepts it and divides each model by its own count.\n\n**Concrete input \u2192 wrong output** (verified with an exact-match grader):\n\n```python\nitems = [\n  {\"correct_answer\":\"X\",\"model_answers\":{\"a\":\"X\",\"b\":\"W\"}},  # a right\n  {\"correct_answer\":\"X\",\"model_answers\":{\"a\":\"W\",\"b\":\"W\"}},  # none right\n  {\"correct_answer\":\"X\",\"model_answers\":{\"c\":\"X\"}},          # only item naming c; c right\n]\n# per_model_accuracy: {'a': 0.5, 'b': 0.0, 'c': 1.0}   -- c graded over 1 item, a/b over 3\n# best_single = 1.0  (c)\n# oracle_any  = 0.667\n# best_single (1.0) > oracle_any (0.667)   <-- IMPOSSIBLE\n```\n\nThe oracle (\"any model correct\") must dominate every single model's accuracy by construction. A ragged pool (a model added/removed between cache builds, or missing cached answers for some items) breaks that and corrupts the R3 verdict table (`ensemble vs best single vs oracle`) that RESULTS.md cites.\n\n**Expected vs actual**\n\n- *Expected:* a ragged pool is rejected (as in the sibling modules), so every model and the oracle share the same denominator and `best_single <= oracle_any` always holds.\n- *Actual:* the ragged pool is graded with per-model denominators and `best_single` can exceed `oracle_any`.\n\n**To reproduce**\n\nRun the snippet above through `analyze(items, benchmark=\"toy\", score_fn=<exact match>)` and observe `best_single > oracle_any`.\n\n**Environment**\n- Python version: 3.10+\n- GPU: not required (pure analysis; inject a `score_fn` for offline runs)\n- Commit hash: current `main`\n",
  "merged_at": "2026-07-14T18:37:43.000Z",
